GPU of the Loongson LS7A1000 bridge (Vivante GC1000) under etnaviv, for
Linux 7.1 (made against 7.1.13; applied by ../install-ls7a.sh to the kernel
source of the running version, from the root of the tree with patch -p1).

etnaviv:
- The bridge's DMA is cache coherent, so GPU writes can stay in the CPU
  caches and a write-combined or uncached CPU mapping reads stale memory:
  readbacks lagged a frame behind or never changed. Buffers are mapped cached
  when dev_is_dma_coherent() (module parameter coherent_cached, default on).
- A dma-buf without struct pages (VRAM shared by loongson, below) is imported
  from its DMA addresses alone; the page array and the core dump skip it.

loongson (the display controller):
- A buffer made in VRAM (every dumb buffer, so every scanout buffer) is
  shared as it is: pinned in VRAM, exported as one DMA range of VRAM. Upstream
  moves every shared buffer to GTT, which the controller cannot scan out, so
  a GPU-drawn frame could not reach the screen without a copy.
- A buffer of this device imported back is the same object (the custom
  dma-buf ops above defeat drm_gem_prime_import()'s check).

